Add an Aerium Guard section to Settings, sharing the first-run presets.

chrome://settings gets a new top-level entry, above Performance in the left
menu: Aerium Guard. It offers the same three setup modes the first-run screen
offers - Recommended, Privacy, Performance - and nothing else.

The point is that the first-run screen is seen exactly once, usually before
anyone has any idea what the tradeoffs mean, and there was no way back to it
short of typing chrome://aerium-first-run from memory. The modes are now
reachable from ordinary Settings, and Settings can also tell you which mode you
are actually on.

The page is about modes only. The individual settings a mode is a shortcut for
stay on the pages they belong to - Privacy and security, Performance, Search -
and are not mirrored here. A pref-bound control shown in two places is two
switches for one setting, with nothing to tell the user which one they last
touched, and the delete-on-exit block in particular reads as a per-page setting
when it is a per-profile one.

One definition of the presets
-----------------------------
The presets are not duplicated. chrome/browser/ui/webui/aerium_presets.h is now
the only place that says what Recommended, Privacy and Performance contain, and
both callers go through it:

  - ungoogled_first_run.h's HandleApplyPreset() shrinks to argument validation
    plus a call to aerium_presets::ApplyPreset(). Every pref write and every
    flag entry moved into the shared header unchanged, along with the comments
    recording where each pref is declared and which enumerator each integer
    literal is.

  - The new settings handler calls the same ApplyPreset(), and additionally
    DetectPreset() to work out which preset the current settings correspond to.

There is no second list in TypeScript. The Aerium Guard page does not know what
any mode contains: it sends a name and renders the answer that comes back. Two
lists that must agree is exactly the shape of bug this avoids.

aerium_presets.h is header-only and entirely inline, so it needs no BUILD.gn
entry - the same reason ungoogled_first_run.h is a header.

Custom
------
DetectPreset() compares the seven prefs a preset writes and returns "custom"
when they match none of the three. The page shows "Current: Custom" and no mode
is marked active, which is the honest answer the moment anyone changes one of
these settings by hand - and they can, from the Privacy, Performance and Search
pages, which is the whole point of a preset being a shortcut rather than a mode
the browser is locked into.

Flags are deliberately not part of detection. SeedFlags() only ever adds entries
and never removes one, precisely so applying a preset cannot undo a flag the
user set on purpose; the consequence is that flags left over from a previously
applied preset say nothing about which preset is in force now, so including them
in the comparison would make detection wrong rather than stricter. The page says
so rather than leaving it to be discovered.

Why the presets need a browser-side handler
-------------------------------------------
The individual settings on the page are ordinary settingsPrivate prefs and need
no handler. The mode presets do: applying one writes seven prefs plus a set of
chrome://flags entries, and browser.enabled_labs_experiments is a local-state
pref that has no business being exposed to the settings WebUI at all. So
AeriumGuardHandler applies and detects in the browser, which is also what keeps
the definitions shared.

It is a SettingsPageUIHandler, registered in settings_ui.cc next to the other
handlers, and is header-only for the same reason as aerium_presets.h.

It also observes the seven prefs with PrefChangeRegistrar and fires
"aerium-guard-preset-changed" when any of them moves, so the displayed mode
updates when the user changes one of these settings somewhere else - which is
precisely when the answer becomes Custom. Observing them here rather than in
TypeScript keeps the list of prefs a preset covers in one place with the rest of
the definition. The profile prefs and the two local-state performance prefs get
one registrar each.

The delete-on-exit block, extracted but still single-homed
----------------------------------------------------------
The delete-on-exit toggle, its eight per-type checkboxes and the never-delete
site list move out of privacy_page.html into their own settings-clear-on-exit
element. Privacy and security remains their only host - they are ordinary prefs
and belong on one page - but they are a self-contained unit of forty lines in a
template that is already long, and privacy_page.ts sheds its now-unused
cr-collapse / settings-checkbox / settings-toggle-button imports as a result.

New settings page mechanics
---------------------------
All the pieces a new top-level section needs, none of which fail loudly if
missed:

  - aerium_guard_page.ts/.html, added to web_component_files in
    chrome/browser/resources/settings/BUILD.gn (which also picks up
    privacy_page/clear_on_exit.ts).
  - A route: r.AERIUM_GUARD = r.BASIC.createSection('/aeriumGuard',
    'aeriumGuard', ...) in route.ts, plus AERIUM_GUARD in the SettingsRoutes
    interface in router.ts. It is deliberately not behind a pageVisibility flag.
  - A <div slot="view" id="aeriumGuard"> in settings_main.html - the id has to
    match the section name passed to createSection(), which is how
    cr-view-manager finds it - and the element imported in settings_main.ts.
  - The menu item in settings_menu.html, after Privacy and security. Its icon
    is <cr-icon icon="settings20:shield">: the shield glyph is declared in the
    settings20 iconset in settings/icons.html, not in the settings one, and
    cr-icon draws nothing at all for an iconset:id pair that does not resolve.
    The AI entry two rows down already uses settings20:magic, so a 20px icon
    among the 24px ones is the menu's own existing practice.
  - Fourteen new IDS_ strings in settings_strings.grdp with their mappings in
    settings_localized_strings_provider.cc, including aeriumGuardPageTitle,
    which route.ts reads through loadTimeData at module load.

The page hosts no writable controls, so it composes no PrefsMixin and
settings_main.html passes it no prefs - the same shape as settings-reset-page
and settings-default-browser-page. It does implement SettingsPlugin's
searchContents(), which every top-level view is required to have or the Settings
search box throws when it reaches this section.

Nothing needs adding to the settingsPrivate allowlist in prefs_util.cc. The page
does not touch settingsPrivate at all: everything on it goes through the handler,
and the preset prefs are written against the real PrefService.
